    <!-- Article Body -->
    <article class="article-body animate-on-scroll">
        <div class="container">

            <p>Since the dawn of time, gardeners have observed the Moon to decide when to sow, plant, prune, or harvest. This ancestral practice, long considered an old wives' tale, is experiencing a spectacular revival among modern gardeners, whether amateur or professional. And for good reason: many practitioners report tangible results when they synchronize their gardening work with lunar cycles. Whether you're skeptical or convinced, this comprehensive guide explains everything you need to know about <strong>moon gardening</strong>, from fundamental principles to practical applications in the vegetable garden, orchard, and ornamental garden. The <strong>lunar garden calendar</strong> will hold no more secrets for you.</p>

            <h2>Understanding the Moon's Cycles</h2>

            <p>To effectively use the <strong>lunar garden calendar</strong>, it's essential to understand the two main cycles that influence plants. Contrary to what many believe, it's not just about the full moon and new moon. Lunar gardening is based on two distinct overlapping phenomena: the synodic cycle (waxing and waning moon) and the sidereal cycle (ascending and descending moon). Confusing these two cycles is the most common mistake beginners make in <strong>moon gardening</strong>.</p>

            <h3>Waxing and waning moon: the synodic cycle</h3>

            <p>The synodic cycle lasts approximately 29.5 days. It corresponds to the visible cycle of the Moon, from new moon to full moon (waxing phase), then from full moon to the next new moon (waning phase). This cycle is the easiest to observe: simply look at the sky in the evening. From new moon to full moon, the illuminated surface increases each night -- that's the waxing moon. From full moon to new moon, the illuminated surface decreases -- that's the waning moon.</p>

            <p>In gardening, distinct properties are attributed to each phase. The <strong>waxing moon</strong> is associated with vitality, resistance, and preservation. Fruits and vegetables harvested during the waxing moon keep longer. Cut flowers last better in a vase. Grafts take more easily. Seeds germinate with more vigor. The <strong>waning moon</strong>, on the other hand, enhances flavors, fragrances, and decomposition. Harvested fruits are more flavorful. Compost decomposes more quickly. Green manures incorporate better into the soil. Pruning wounds heal faster. It's also the ideal time for natural treatments as plants absorb preparations better.</p>

            <div class="info-box">
                <h4>Memory trick</h4>
                <p>To tell if the moon is waxing or waning without a calendar, observe it in the sky. If by imagining a vertical bar against the crescent you form the letter "p" (as in "premier"/first), the moon is in its first quarter, therefore waxing. If you form the letter "d" (as in "dernier"/last), it's in its last quarter, therefore waning. Note: this trick only works in the Northern Hemisphere.</p>
            </div>

            <h3>Ascending and descending moon: the sidereal cycle</h3>

            <p>The sidereal cycle lasts approximately 27.3 days. It corresponds to the Moon's movement in the sky relative to the horizon. For about 13 days, the Moon rises a little higher in the sky each day -- that's the <strong>ascending moon</strong>. For the following 13 days, it drops a little lower -- that's the <strong>descending moon</strong>. Important: this phenomenon has nothing to do with the waxing or waning moon. You can very well have a waxing and descending moon at the same time, or a waning and ascending moon.</p>

            <p>During the <strong>ascending moon</strong>, sap rises into the aerial parts of plants. This is the prime time for sowing, grafting, harvesting fruits and aerial parts, and gathering medicinal and aromatic herbs. Seeds benefit from upward sap flow that promotes germination and growth of stems and leaves. During the <strong>descending moon</strong>, sap flows back down toward the roots. This is the ideal time for planting, transplanting, taking cuttings, pruning, enriching the soil, working compost, and harvesting root vegetables. Transplanted plants root better because energy is concentrated in the root system.</p>

            <h3>How to distinguish ascending from descending moon</h3>

            <p>To determine if the moon is ascending or descending, observe its position in the sky at the same time on two consecutive evenings. If it's higher on the second evening, it's ascending. If it's lower, it's descending. In practice, most gardeners refer to a <strong>lunar garden calendar</strong> that precisely indicates these phases day by day, as direct observation requires rigor and good weather conditions. Many free lunar calendars are available online and in gardening magazines.</p>

