            <h2>The Four Types of Lunar Days</h2>

            <p>Beyond the Moon's phases, lunar gardening takes into account the Moon's position in the zodiac. During its 27.3-day sidereal cycle, the Moon passes through the 12 zodiac constellations. Each constellation is associated with one of four elements -- Earth, Water, Air, Fire -- and by extension with one of four plant parts: roots, leaves, flowers, and fruits. This classification defines the four types of <strong>leaf, flower, fruit, and root days</strong> that are at the heart of the lunar gardening calendar.</p>

            <h3>Root days: when the Moon passes Earth constellations</h3>

            <p>The Earth constellations are Taurus, Virgo, and Capricorn. When the Moon transits one of these constellations, energy favors roots and everything that grows underground. This is the ideal time for sowing, planting, transplanting, hilling, and harvesting root vegetables: carrots, turnips, beets, radishes, potatoes, onions, garlic, shallots, parsnips, Jerusalem artichokes, salsify, and celeriac. Root days are also favorable for soil preparation, tilling, adding compost, and organic amendments. If you need to weed, also prioritize root days: pulled weeds are less likely to regrow.</p>

            <h3>Leaf days: when the Moon passes Water constellations</h3>

            <p>The Water constellations are Cancer, Scorpio, and Pisces. These days promote leaf and stem growth. This is the time to sow, plant, transplant, and harvest all leafy vegetables: lettuce, spinach, cabbage, leeks, celery, chard, corn salad, arugula, parsley, chives, chervil, and all herbs grown for their leaves. Leaf days are also indicated for mowing the lawn if you want it to grow back thick and vigorous. It's also a good time to prepare and apply plant teas (nettle, comfrey, horsetail) that stimulate foliage growth.</p>

            <h3>Flower days: when the Moon passes Air constellations</h3>

            <p>The Air constellations are Gemini, Libra, and Aquarius. Energy is directed toward flowers and everything related to plant beauty and reproduction. This is the time to sow, plant, transplant, take cuttings, and divide flowering plants: roses, peonies, dahlias, geraniums, petunias, lavender, begonias, chrysanthemums, and all flowering annuals and perennials. Flower days also suit flower vegetables like cauliflower, broccoli, and artichoke. Cut your flowers on flower days and during the waxing moon: they'll last much longer in a vase. It's also the ideal time to harvest edible flowers like nasturtium, borage, or pansy.</p>

            <h3>Fruit days: when the Moon passes Fire constellations</h3>

            <p>The Fire constellations are Aries, Leo, and Sagittarius. Energy favors fruits and seeds. This is the time to sow, plant, prune, and harvest fruit vegetables and fruit trees: tomatoes, zucchini, cucumbers, eggplant, peppers, pumpkins, squash, melons, watermelons, strawberries, raspberries, currants, apple trees, pear trees, cherry trees, and plum trees. Fruit days are also best for harvesting seeds intended for next year's sowing. If you need to prune your fruit trees, do it on fruit days during the descending moon to encourage fruit production. Fruit days also suit cereals and legumes grown for their seeds: dried beans, peas, broad beans, lentils.</p>

            <div class="info-box">
                <h4>What about lunar nodes?</h4>
                <p>About twice a month, the Moon crosses Earth's orbit around the Sun. These crossing points are called lunar nodes. According to lunar gardening tradition, it's best to do nothing in the garden 4 to 5 hours before and after a lunar node. Similarly, perigee days (Moon closest to Earth) and apogee days (Moon farthest away) are considered unfavorable. These times are indicated in good lunar calendars with a rest symbol.</p>
            </div>

            <h2>Practical Application: The Lunar Calendar in the Vegetable Garden</h2>

            <p>Now that you've mastered the theoretical principles, let's see how to concretely apply the <strong>lunar garden calendar</strong> to your vegetable garden, month by month and vegetable by vegetable. The goal is not to become a slave to the calendar, but to use it as an additional tool to optimize your crops. If weather conditions are favorable on a root day but you need to sow your lettuce, go ahead anyway. Common sense always takes priority over the lunar calendar.</p>

            <h3>Sowing during the ascending moon</h3>

            <p>The golden rule of lunar sowing is simple: sow during the ascending moon. During this phase, sap naturally rises into the aerial parts of plants, promoting germination and the emergence of seedlings above the soil. Seeds sown during the ascending moon germinate faster and produce more vigorous seedlings, according to many gardeners' observations. Combine the ascending moon with the type of day corresponding to the vegetable you're sowing: root days for carrots, leaf days for lettuce, fruit days for tomatoes, flower days for cauliflower.</p>

            <p>Concretely, if you want to sow carrots, look for a root day during the ascending moon in your lunar calendar. If you want to sow tomatoes, find a fruit day during the ascending moon. For lettuce, a leaf day during the ascending moon. This optimal combination isn't always possible -- sometimes the day type and lunar phase don't coincide with your schedule or the weather. In that case, prioritize the ascending moon first, then the day type if possible.</p>

            <h3>Planting and transplanting during the descending moon</h3>

            <p>For planting, transplanting, or taking cuttings, prioritize the descending moon. During this phase, sap flows back down toward the roots, promoting root development. A tomato plant transplanted during the descending moon will develop a denser, deeper root system than one transplanted during the ascending moon. Again, combine with the appropriate day type: transplanting lettuce on leaf days and descending moon, planting potatoes on root days and descending moon, setting out tomato plants on fruit days and descending moon.</p>

            <h3>Harvesting according to the Moon</h3>

            <p>The timing of harvest is just as important as that of sowing. For vegetables intended for storage (potatoes, onions, garlic, winter carrots, squash), harvest during the waxing moon: they'll keep longer. For vegetables meant to be consumed quickly and that should be flavorful (tomatoes, strawberries, herbs, salads), harvest during the waning moon: flavors and aromas will be more pronounced. Fruits harvested during the waxing moon for making jams yield better results as sugar binds better. Medicinal and aromatic plants harvested during the ascending moon on flower days concentrate more active compounds.</p>

