            <h2>The Lunar Calendar in the Orchard</h2>

            <p>The orchard is an area where the <strong>lunar garden calendar</strong> finds particularly interesting applications, especially for pruning, grafting, and fruit harvesting.</p>

            <h3>Pruning fruit trees</h3>

            <p>Pruning fruit trees is ideally done during the descending and waning moon. With sap concentrated in the roots, pruning causes less stress and sap loss. Pruning wounds heal faster. For training young trees, choose a fruit day during the descending moon. For maintenance pruning of mature trees, a fruit day during the descending and waning moon is optimal. Absolutely avoid pruning during frost or lunar nodes. For stone fruit trees (cherry, plum, peach), prune after harvest in summer, always during the descending moon to limit gum flow.</p>

            <h3>Grafting during the ascending moon</h3>

            <p>Grafting is an operation where lunar timing seems particularly decisive. Always graft during the ascending moon: rising sap promotes the union of the scion with the rootstock. Choose a fruit day for fruit trees. Success rates observed by lunar gardening practitioners are significantly better during the ascending moon. Spring, when sap begins actively circulating, is the ideal season. For bud grafting, practice in late summer, always during the ascending moon on fruit days.</p>

            <h3>Fruit harvest and storage</h3>

            <p>For fruits meant to be eaten fresh within the following days, harvest during the waning moon on fruit days: aromas will be at their peak. For fruits intended for storage (winter apples and pears, walnuts, hazelnuts), harvest during the waxing moon on fruit days: they'll keep for months without issue. For fruits meant for processing (jams, compotes, juices, dried fruits), the waxing moon gives better results for jams and drying, while the waning moon is preferable for juices and compotes, as fruits release more juice.</p>

            <h2>The Lunar Calendar in the Ornamental Garden</h2>

            <p>Ornamental plants respond to the same lunar principles as vegetable plants. Here's how to optimize your ornamental garden care with <strong>moon gardening</strong>.</p>

            <h3>Planting and dividing perennials</h3>

            <p>Plant, divide, and transplant your perennials during the descending moon to promote rooting. Flower days are ideal for plants grown for their blooms (peonies, irises, daylilies, asters), while leaf days are better suited for plants grown for their foliage (hostas, ferns, ornamental grasses). Roses are planted on flower days during the descending moon, ideally in November or March. Spring bulbs (tulips, daffodils, crocuses) are planted on flower days during the descending moon in autumn. Summer bulbs (dahlias, gladioli, lilies) are planted in spring under the same conditions.</p>

            <h3>Mowing the lawn</h3>

            <p>Lawn mowing also follows lunar rhythms. For a thick, vigorous lawn that grows back quickly, mow during the ascending moon on leaf days: rising sap stimulates regrowth. For a lawn that grows back more slowly and requires less frequent mowing, mow during the descending moon: growth will be slowed. This tip is particularly useful in midsummer when frequent mowing becomes a chore. In autumn, a final mow during the descending moon helps the lawn stay neat longer through winter.</p>

            <h3>Taking cuttings and layering</h3>

            <p>Taking cuttings is done during the descending moon to promote root formation. Choose the day type corresponding to the plant: flower days for roses, hydrangeas, and lavender; leaf days for boxwood and conifers; fruit days for currant and blackcurrant bushes. Layering follows the same principles. For hardwood cuttings in winter, the descending and waning moon gives the best results. For softwood cuttings in spring and summer, the descending moon is sufficient regardless of the synodic cycle.</p>

            <blockquote>
                <p>"The gardener who observes the Moon is simply doing what their ancestors did for millennia: listening to nature's rhythms and attuning to them. It's neither magic nor superstition -- it's attention paid to the living world."</p>
            </blockquote>

            <h2>Month-by-Month Lunar Calendar: Essential Tasks</h2>

            <p>Here is a concise guide to the work to be done each month according to lunar phases. This calendar is indicative and should be adapted to your region and climate.</p>

            <h3>January - February: planning</h3>

            <p>This is the time to order your seeds, plan your sowings, and prepare your tools. During the ascending moon on fruit days, sow under heated cover tomatoes, peppers, and eggplant (February). On leaf days during the ascending moon, sow spring lettuce under cold frames. During the descending moon, prune pome fruit trees (apple, pear) and small fruit bushes. Take advantage of root days during the descending moon to add compost and amendments to the vegetable garden soil. January is also the ideal month for forcing endives and rhubarb in the cellar.</p>

            <h3>March - April: the big start</h3>

            <p>The vegetable garden awakens. During the ascending moon on root days, sow carrots, radishes, turnips, and beets directly in the ground once the soil has warmed. On leaf days during the ascending moon, sow spinach, arugula, mesclun, and lettuce. On fruit days during the ascending moon, sow zucchini, cucumbers, and melons under cover. During the descending moon, transplant seedlings sown in February. Plant potatoes on root days during the descending moon (traditionally around March 19). Divide perennials on flower days during the descending moon. Plant bare-root roses before the end of March.</p>

            <h3>May - June: abundance</h3>

            <p>After the last frosts, plant tomato, zucchini, pepper, and eggplant seedlings outdoors on fruit days during the descending moon. Sow beans on fruit days during the ascending moon. On leaf days during the ascending moon, sow summer salads, basil, parsley, and cilantro. On flower days during the descending moon, plant summer annuals: petunias, impatiens, begonias, marigolds. Harvest strawberries on fruit days for optimal preservation. Hill potatoes on root days during the descending moon. Pinch off tomato suckers on fruit days.</p>

            <h3>July - August: harvests</h3>

            <p>This is the period of abundance. Harvest regularly following the corresponding days: fruit days for tomatoes, zucchini, cucumbers, and green beans; root days for garlic, onions, and shallots; leaf days for salads and herbs. During the waxing moon, harvest for storage. During the waning moon, harvest for immediate flavor. Sow fall and winter vegetables during the ascending moon: storage carrots, turnips, black radishes, corn salad, winter spinach. On flower days, harvest lavender flowers for scented sachets. Trim hedges during the descending moon to slow regrowth.</p>

            <h3>September - October: winter preparations</h3>

            <p>During the descending moon on root days, plant fall garlic and shallots. On flower days during the descending moon, plant spring bulbs: tulips, daffodils, crocuses, hyacinths, grape hyacinths. Sow green manures (mustard, phacelia, rye) during the ascending moon to cover bare soil through winter. Harvest squash, pumpkins, and apples on fruit days during the waxing moon for long-term storage. Divide irises, peonies, and daylilies on flower days during the descending moon. Prepare hardwood cuttings during the descending moon. Begin the big vegetable garden cleanup during the descending moon.</p>

            <h3>November - December: rest</h3>

            <p>During the descending moon, plant bare-root fruit trees and shrubs. Prune pome fruit trees outside of frost on fruit days during the descending moon. Add compost and well-rotted manure on root days during the descending moon: they'll have all winter to enrich the soil. Protect frost-sensitive plants with thick mulch, ideally during the descending moon. On leaf days during the ascending moon, force endives and chicory in the cellar. Take advantage of the quiet period to maintain your tools, plan next year's vegetable garden, and study your lunar calendar.</p>

